course: dictionary
lesson: 3978
title: One word, many meanings: gain
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word gain has several meanings, and the words around it show you which one the writer means. Look for clues such as weight, yards, money or a race.
explain: «A newborn blue whale can gain about 90 kilograms a day.» Here gain means to get more weight. The calf is growing, so it is getting heavier.
explain: «The attack has 4 downs to gain 10 yards.» In sport, gain means to move forward and get more ground. In a race, to gain on someone means to get closer to them.
explain: Try a quick check. Swap in get more or win, and see if the sentence still makes sense. If it does, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Gain means to get more of something, like weight, points, speed or money.
fact: To gain something can also mean to win or earn it, like gaining a friend or gaining skill.
fact: A gain is an increase, or something you get. In money, a gain is a profit: you sell something for more than it cost.
fact: «Gain» can be a verb (to gain, gains, gained, gaining) or a noun (a gain, gains).
fact: In maths, gain is the opposite of loss. Phrases to know: «gain weight», «gain on», «gain time», «no pain, no gain».
exercise: «A newborn blue whale can gain about 90 kilograms a day.» What does gain mean here? | *To get more weight, growing heavier | To lose weight | To swim faster | To sleep
exercise: «The attack has 4 downs to gain 10 yards.» What does gain mean in this sentence? | To count the score | To run backwards | To give up the ball | *To move forward and get more ground
exercise: Ben bought a bike for £25 and sold it for £40. What is his gain in money? | £25, the cost price | £65 | *£15, his profit | He lost £15
test: «The rusty nail actually weighs more, because it has gained oxygen from the air.» What has the nail done? | Lost its oxygen | Shrunk in the sun | *Got extra oxygen from the air | Turned into water
test: Sam says gain only means winning a prize. What is the best answer? | No, gain means to fall asleep | Yes, gain only means winning a prize | *No, gain can also mean to get more, like weight or points, or to get closer to someone in a race | Yes, gain means to lose something
exam: Which sentence uses gain as a noun, meaning an increase? | Gain the prize by trying hard. | *The company made a gain of ten per cent. | We need to gain on the runner ahead. | The team will gain ten points tonight.
